{"kind":"assert","text":"Promise Theory alignment findings (Burgess, Cooperation in Human and Machine Agents, arXiv:2604.10505), operator-reviewed 2026-08-07, as review inputs to the constraint-algebra enumeration and starter kind-definitions. (1) Satisfier: originating-requester instantiates the downstream principle - the agent on the receiving end of a commitment holds the assessment that closes it; record this rationale in the ratified enumeration so a later offer/accept polarity pair (an offer is a +promise, its acceptance the -promise, the acceptor the satisfier) extends the same family instead of minting a new concept. A dangling free-standing promise is an offer nobody has accepted. (2) Lifecycle terminals: satisfied, reneged, released, withdrawn, with the fault line normative - released (upstream commitment or condition died; kept faith) must stay distinguishable from reneged (performer broke a live commitment), and release/staleness flow only along governing/conditioning basis edges, joining the governs-vs-cites remainder of assert 2503e6de. (3) Algebra smallness: the small-language/large-language trade is an independent argument for a purpose-built finite algebra over a schema-language subset (open question 1); guidance prose stays powerless and misunderstanding is repaired by further acts, never richer types. (4) Guidance prose: ratify and dissent are one family - per-agent assessments differing only in authority; an unauthorized ratify is a visible ineffective assessment. Synthesis: gitseq keeps the LAP loop as the shape of coordination and adopts Promise Theory on its force - every commitment voluntary, binding only its signer, assessed downstream, dying without fault when its conditions die.","body":{"reference":"arXiv:2604.10505 (hint; claims summarized above are what this assert stands on)"}}